WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.824
iOS Safari caps

00:00:01.635 --> 00:00:02.354
your web app

00:00:02.213 --> 00:00:03.581
at 60 FPS

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.796
On 120 hertz

00:00:05.608 --> 00:00:06.433
iPhones

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:09.003
WebKit limits requestAnimationFrame

00:00:09.004 --> 00:00:10.029
and touch events

00:00:09.841 --> 00:00:10.996
to 60 hertz

00:00:11.103 --> 00:00:12.058
which makes even

00:00:11.869 --> 00:00:13.367
great mobile websites

00:00:13.178 --> 00:00:14.263
feel sluggish

00:00:14.782 --> 00:00:16.102
Rida F'kih's trick

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:16.633
is to use

00:00:16.445 --> 00:00:17.883
native scroll areas

00:00:17.966 --> 00:00:18.697
which run on

00:00:18.508 --> 00:00:19.746
the compositor thread

00:00:19.923 --> 00:00:20.831
as the input

00:00:20.643 --> 00:00:22.152
source for animations

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:23.978
Scroll events only

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:24.769
arrived 5 to

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:25.700
26 times a

00:00:25.512 --> 00:00:26.879
second during momentum

00:00:27.057 --> 00:00:27.905
so he built

00:00:27.729 --> 00:00:28.990
a separate controller

00:00:28.802 --> 00:00:29.733
to read scroll

00:00:29.545 --> 00:00:30.842
position fast enough

00:00:30.654 --> 00:00:31.679
to feel native

00:00:32.214 --> 00:00:33.228
Full story on

00:00:33.039 --> 00:00:33.935
the dashboard
